  "rendered": "The sodiation of the carbon layer provides a small capacity of 0.05 mAh/cm^2[1] (or 120 mAh/g[2]), as read from the sodiation curve in Fig. 1[3]. The paper calls this negligible[4] compared with the plating capacities. Those plating capacities run from 0.25 mAh/cm^2[5] (the loading used for the impedance tests) through 0.5 mAh/cm^2[6] and 8 mAh/cm^2[7] in the rate and loading tests shown in Fig. 2[8], up to 12 mAh/cm^2[9] in the extended cycling test. The carbon sodiation capacity is therefore far smaller than the plated sodium capacity, which the paper identifies as the meaningful capacity: the carbon layer's contribution is only a small fraction of the maximum plated loading.",
